{:edb/db-before-t 1149 :edb/db-after-t 1150 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534462 50 #inst "2026-10-11T21:24:56.809Z" 13194139534462 17592186045417 true] [17592186064888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186063384 13194139534462 17592186045417 false] [17592186064888 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534462 17592186045417 true] [17592186064888 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$ \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+x, & x<-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+1, & x\\ge -1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534462 17592186045417 false] [17592186069334 1068 #edb/ref 17592186067174 13194139534462 17592186045417 false] [17592186069334 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+3 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-2}+1 \\\\ -2x+2 & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ (-2x+2)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ 2(-x+1)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ (-x+1)(x-2) & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x+3$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fee04552a47d93f1dbd788e69e12c4b58e377ec4c0b63c3ed55c712d337f94.png)" 13194139534462 17592186045417 true] [17592186069334 1074 "We need to solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x+3 &  \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get a quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+3 & =\\frac{2}{x-2}+1 \\\\ -2x+2 & =\\frac{2}{x-2} \\\\ (-2x+2)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ 2(-x+1)(x-2) & =2 \\\\ (-x+1)(x-2) & =1 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+3x-2 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{{3}^{2}-4(1)(3)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{9-12}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\pm \\sqrt{-3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe square root of a negative number is not a real number, so there are no real solutions. Consequently, the line $y=-2x+3$ does not intersect the hyperbola $y=\\frac{2}{x-2}+1.$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fee04552a47d93f1dbd788e69e12c4b58e377ec4c0b63c3ed55c712d337f94.png)" 13194139534462 17592186045417 false] [17592186129481 1068 #edb/ref 17592186126386 13194139534462 17592186045417 false] [17592186129481 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=3i+2j,\\,u=-7i-6j,\\,v=2i\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 2i & =(-7i-6j)+(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =3ti+2tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9=3t \\\\ 6=2t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139534462 17592186045417 true] [17592186129481 1074 "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=3i+2j,\\,u=-7i-6j,\\,v=2i\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 2i & =(-7i-6j)+(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =(3i+2j)t \\\\ 9i+6j & =3ti+2tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}9=3t \\\\ 6=2t\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,t=3\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139534462 17592186045417 false] [17592186198778 1068 #edb/ref 17592186195379 13194139534462 17592186045417 false] [17592186198778 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B=\\{(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times N\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}=\\{(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f8808e4c35775c754dab0bf328a8d129e423009e18ec5f4b962461eba2afcea2.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $2.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}=\\{(x,2)\\,:\\,x\\in R\\}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/a5/a51fe9dc5bf7c55b01f72a45d4b88bde4653f0d3da85c1231b0d54c76a6e8c81.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7a/7ae3c488e60d3dcda304649e575a7d2efc54be7262a54d0455550af420c39b68.png)" 13194139534462 17592186045417 true] [17592186198778 1074 "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS=R\\times N\n$$\n\n \ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a real number and whose $y$-coordinate is a natural number.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in R,\\,y\\in N}\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S,$ consider ${S}_{1}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $1.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{1}={(x,1)\\,:\\,x\\in R}\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f8808e4c35775c754dab0bf328a8d129e423009e18ec5f4b962461eba2afcea2.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{2}⊂S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate is any real number and whose $y$-coordinate equals $2.$\n\n$$\n{S}_{2}={(x,2)\\,:\\,x\\in R}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/a5/a51fe9dc5bf7c55b01f72a45d4b88bde4653f0d3da85c1231b0d54c76a6e8c81.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets ${S}_{3},{S}_{4},{S}_{5},…$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\times N & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup … \\\\  & ={⋃}_{i\\in N}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $R\\times N$ as the set of horizontal lines shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7a/7ae3c488e60d3dcda304649e575a7d2efc54be7262a54d0455550af420c39b68.png)" 13194139534462 17592186045417 false]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
